{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>חָמָס</b> — this is robbery and oppression. And He gave Noah the reason as חָמָס and did not mention the corruption of the way, because חָמָס is the well-known and publicized sin. And our Sages said (Sanhedrin 108a) that their decree was sealed on account of it. And the reason is because it is a rational commandment — there is no need for a prophet to warn them concerning it. And furthermore, that it is evil toward the heavens and toward created beings. And behold, He informed Noah of the sin on account of which the end has come — the alarm has sounded." "<b>וְהִנְנִי מַשְׁחִיתָם אֶת הָאָרֶץ</b> — like 'from the earth,' and likewise 'as I go out of the city' (Exodus 9:29), 'his feet became diseased' (1 Kings 15:23). Another interpretation: 'אֶת הָאָרֶץ' — together with the earth, for even three handbreadths of the plowshare were dissolved; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 6:13) from Genesis Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 31:7). And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 6:13) that the word 'מַשְׁחִיתָם' draws itself and another along with it — 'and behold I am destroying them and destroying the earth.' And by way of truth, it is like 'אֵת הַשָּׁמַיִם וְאֵת הָאָרֶץ' (Genesis 1:1), for the earth will be in corruption — through the corruption of the earth they shall be corrupted, and they are corrupted in the world to come, in the manner of 'and He was grieved to His heart' (Genesis 6:6). And to this they alluded in Genesis Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 31:7) — to the son of a lord who had a wet-nurse: as long as he was soiling himself, his nurse would be afflicted, and so forth."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The phrase 'the corruption of the way' (הַשְׁחָתַת הַדֶּרֶךְ) echoes Genesis 6:12; 'a rational commandment' (מִצְוָה מֻשְׂכֶּלֶת) renders the philosophic term for a law knowable by reason.", 1 "The cryptic final kabbalistic passage about 'the son of a lord who had a wet-nurse' is rendered hyper-literally; Ramban cites the midrashic parable as a hint (רָמְזוּ) at a deeper sod teaching left deliberately obscure. The phrase 'draws itself and another along with it' translates the grammatical term מוֹשֶׁכֶת עַצְמָהּ וְאַחֶרֶת, referring to an accusative particle that governs two objects."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:20:49.337684738Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}